---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/browse-abandonment';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/browse-abandonment');
const desc = 'Evaluate browse-abandonment messaging through permission, identity, product-event quality, journey exits and incremental contribution.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/use-cases" class="text-sm font-bold text-orange-700 hover:text-slate-950">← All use cases</a>
      <p class="badge mt-6">Browse abandonment</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">SMS marketing apps for browse abandonment</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-orange-950/80">A product view is evidence of a visit, not a request for a text. Build a browse journey only when you can connect useful product context to a permitted recipient and stop it when the situation changes.</p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps">Explore app profiles</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">Coordinate email and SMS</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Separate tracking permission, identity and messaging permission</h2>
      <p>Treat these as independent gates. Permission to collect browsing events does not establish permission to send promotional SMS. A stored phone number, email subscription or previous order is not by itself the SMS permission record for this journey. Preserve the capture source, timestamp, disclosure version, channel and scope, together with subsequent withdrawals. Apply the requirements for the destination and message purpose; this workflow is not a legal assurance. See <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet hours</a>.</p>
      <p>For an anonymous visitor, retain only the data your tracking rules permit and do not invent a reachable profile. For a known subscriber, require a supported link between the event and the permitted destination. A cookie, shared tablet, forwarded link or household phone can connect activity to the wrong person. Record identity provenance and confidence; quarantine conflicting merges rather than silently transferring browsing history or permission. Logging in later should not automatically release every old anonymous event.</p>
      <p>Keep product details out of message copy when the recipient relationship or browsing context is uncertain. For sensitive categories, an inferred interest may be unsuitable even with a technically valid match. If you cannot establish eligibility, suppress the journey rather than asking a guessed number to confirm its interest.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Make the event useful before choosing a delay</h2>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://shopify.dev/docs/api/web-pixels-api/standard-events/product_viewed">Shopify's product_viewed reference</a> describes a product-details-page visit event with event ID, timestamp, client ID and product-variant data. That event definition does not establish purchase intent, a verified phone identity or permission to message. Your integration still needs to establish what it actually captured.</p>
      <p>Keep event time separate from arrival time, and retain source, schema version, product ID, variant ID, market and currency where available. Reject malformed records, test traffic and unsupported mappings. Deduplicate repeated delivery of the same source event; separately decide how repeated genuine visits update one active interest episode. A default variant in a page event need not mean the visitor deliberately selected that size or color.</p>
      <p>A quick visit may be research, a gift search or an accidental click. Repeated views may be refreshes or a shared device. Define the evidence that makes a reminder useful for your catalog and measure its false positives. There is no universal pageview count or waiting period that establishes intent. Choose a bounded inactivity window and hard expiry for the proposed journey, then test them; do not turn every page visit into a send.</p>
      <p>At dispatch, resolve the current product URL, variant availability, price, currency and destination market. Do not substitute another size merely because the viewed variant sold out. If variant selection is uncertain, avoid variant-specific claims. Remove a stale price or cancel the job when current product data cannot support the message. Avoid automatic discounts: helpful sizing, compatibility or product information may be more appropriate, if the channel and recipient are eligible.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Let stronger events and customer needs end the browse job</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Cart or checkout:</strong> retire the relevant browse episode and evaluate the separate <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">cart journey</a>. Do not let both queues claim the same reminder slot. A cart event is not permission to send either message.</li>
        <li><strong>Purchase:</strong> cancel reminders for the purchased interest and apply the defined post-purchase pause across journeys. Reconcile late orders before dispatch. An unrelated purchase may justify a broader pause; document that scope instead of assuming every order settles every interest.</li>
        <li><strong>Support:</strong> pause promotions when a relevant complaint, return or unresolved product question makes a reminder inappropriate. A service response must not become a route around promotional restrictions. Closing the ticket does not automatically revive an expired job.</li>
        <li><strong>Withdrawal:</strong> stop the affected channel and purpose according to the request, preserving the suppression record through exports and migrations. Do not treat every SMS withdrawal as an email withdrawal, or move a blocked promotion to another channel without checking that channel's permission and request scope.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Choose one owner for each contact's promotional opportunity across welcome, browse, cart, launch and other journeys. Reserve that slot atomically before dispatch across tools, with channel and combined exposure limits. If another journey wins, cancel or defer browse only within its original validity window. Recheck time-zone evidence, current sending restrictions, support status, permission and purchase state after any delay. Unknown or stale state should hold dispatch.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Prevent old events from becoming new messages</h2>
      <p>Use a stable event deduplication key and a separate message key for the recipient, interest episode, channel and journey version. Track queued, reserved, submitted, accepted, failed and cancelled states. Multiple providers need a shared exposure ledger; local frequency settings alone cannot arbitrate simultaneous sends. Keep a monotonic cancellation or suppression version so an older event cannot reopen an exited episode.</p>
      <p>Set a hard expiry based on the original qualifying event. A quiet-hours delay, integration outage, retry or deployment must not reset that clock. If a provider times out after submission, reconcile its acceptance state using the same idempotency key or delivery record before retrying. When acceptance remains uncertain and safe reconciliation is unavailable, hold for investigation rather than issue a fresh send. Do not promise recall after provider acceptance; record the cancellation boundary and investigate late exits.</p>
      <p>After an outage, replay events into a non-sending reconciliation path first. Apply purchases, withdrawals, identity corrections and support exits before considering still-valid jobs. Inspect event age and stale mappings, bound the recovery cohort, and enable a stop switch for abnormal queue age or suppression failures. Recovery should restore present eligibility, not empty a backlog into subscribers' phones.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Proposed acceptance fixtures</h2>
      <p>These scenarios are explicitly hypothetical proposed acceptance fixtures, not merchant results or claims that any app already implements the controls.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Anonymous research:</strong> a visitor views a product several times with tracking allowed but no verified messaging destination. Expected: no SMS job; later identification requires a fresh eligibility decision.</li>
        <li><strong>Shared tablet:</strong> a permitted subscriber's device switches accounts. Expected: conflicting identity suspends personalization; no transfer of the first account's permission to the second.</li>
        <li><strong>Variant ambiguity:</strong> a product event carries the default small size while the visitor's selected size is unknown. Expected: no assertion that small was chosen; an unavailable selected variant cancels variant-specific copy.</li>
        <li><strong>Late purchase:</strong> an order arrives after browse is queued but before submission. Expected: exit wins and cancels the job. If arrival is after provider acceptance, record the race and prevent subsequent reminders rather than claiming the first was recalled.</li>
        <li><strong>Competing cart and support:</strong> a cart event and open complaint arrive together. Expected: browse retires; service priority prevents a cart promotion from bypassing the pause.</li>
        <li><strong>Withdrawal during deferral:</strong> a queued text waits for an eligible sending period, then receives an SMS withdrawal. Expected: cancellation survives the scheduler restart; email is evaluated separately under the request's scope.</li>
        <li><strong>Replay and uncertain submission:</strong> a duplicate event returns after a timeout. Expected: one logical message key, provider-state reconciliation, and no blind retry or expiry extension.</li>
        <li><strong>Outage recovery:</strong> a backlog contains an expired product event and a later purchase. Expected: reconciliation applies the exit without sending; only unexpired, currently eligible episodes enter a bounded restart.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Evaluate tools against the missing control</h2>
      <p>An existing unified stack can be a useful starting point if it exposes reliable product events, channel-specific permission, cross-journey exclusions and dispatch records. A dedicated SMS layer can fit if it can consume the same current exits and participate in shared exposure arbitration. A separate email layer can fit longer product education when email permission exists and its jobs respect the same episode ownership. These are conditional architecture choices, not supplier rankings.</p>
      <p>Use the profiles for <a class="link" href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a>, <a class="link" href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a>, <a class="link" href="/apps/postscript">Postscript</a>, <a class="link" href="/apps/attentive">Attentive</a> and <a class="link" href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy</a> to build a shortlist. Ask each candidate to demonstrate your fixtures with its current plan and integration. Establish event coverage, permission fields, cancellation latency, dedupe scope, exportability, retention and recovery behavior before assigning ownership. Get current cost terms for actual eligible volume and integration work; a vendor name does not establish any of these capabilities.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Measure incremental contribution, including people who do not buy</h2>
      <p>Assign eligible identities to stable randomized treatment and holdout groups before dispatch. Keep the holdout free of this browse intervention across tools; define which other journeys remain identical in both arms. If identity changes can move people between arms, resolve that contamination before interpreting results. A holdout for one message estimates that message policy, not the effect of the entire marketing stack.</p>
      <p>Use a prespecified observation window long enough to include normal purchase and refund lag. Compare contribution per assigned eligible customer, including non-buyers and failed sends. Start with net sales after discounts and refunds, subtract product, fulfillment, payment, messaging and variable support costs, and avoid counting discounts or refunds twice. Report the treatment-minus-holdout difference with uncertainty; keep rollout subscription and setup costs separate when deciding whether to scale.</p>
      <p>For a hypothetical arithmetic example, treatment contribution of $1,200 across 1,000 assigned customers is $1.20 each; holdout contribution of $1,000 across 1,000 is $1.00 each. The observed difference is $0.20 per eligible customer before incremental fixed costs. That point estimate alone does not establish lift. Examine uncertainty, delayed purchases, stock competition, opt-outs, complaints and return rates. Platform-attributed orders can include customers who would have bought anyway.</p>
      <p>Review <a class="link" href="/use-cases/analytics">analytics</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/offer-discipline">offer discipline</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a> before expanding the audience. Scale only when the observed benefit and operational reliability justify the additional exposure.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
